`trigger` calls `fixEvent(event)` which, when `event` is falsey **AND** the trigger is called as a result of an event handler **AND** that event handler was for a real DOM-event, returns the _currently active event._ This has the effect of causing "infinite" recursion and exceeding the call-stack limit.

Troublesome spot:
https://github.com/videojs/video.js/blob/master/src/js/utils/events.js#L227-L228

Example:

``` javascript
player.on('pause', function () {
  player.trigger(undefined);
});
```

..then pause the player and you will get the stack size exceeded error.

Whereas this example **does not trigger** the issue since the event is not a proper DOM originating event:

``` javascript
player.on('foo', function () {
  player.trigger(undefined);
});
player.trigger('foo');
```

