Two component instances cannot bind the same prototype method(s) to player events without clobbering each other. I discovered this while refactoring a plugin. [Here's a JSBin to demonstrate the issue.](http://jsbin.com/fabopi/edit?html,js,console,output)

There are two ways to work around this:
1. Use `Function.prototype.bind` to create an instance-bound copy of the listener function:

   ``` js
   this.listener = this.listener.bind(this);
   this.on(this.player(), 'eventname', this.listener);
   ```
2. Use an anonymous function that calls the prototype method (assuming ES6):

   ``` js
   this.listener = (e) => Foo.prototype.listener.call(this, e);
   this.on(this.player(), 'eventname', this.listener);
   ```

I tried `videojs.bind` as well, but the GUID magic is part of the problem - it is considered the same function if it has the same GUID; so, as soon as the first instance passes it to `videojs.bind` it is always and forever "the same function" for the purposes of video.js' event-binding behavior. The other part of the problem is that `on` uses the target element for tracking purposes rather than the object doing the binding.

